  "slides": [
    {
      "page_title": "Why Rate Limit At All",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Two Words, One Job",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Rate limit = cap on ops per unit time",
            "Throttling = the action taken when cap breached",
            "Limiter decides; throttler enforces"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Four Driving Motives"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Motive",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Capacity",
              "avoid overload collapse"
            ],
            [
              "Cost",
              "per-call cloud/LLM spend"
            ],
            [
              "Fairness",
              "one tenant starves rest"
            ],
            [
              "Abuse",
              "cred stuffing, scraping, spam"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. The Retry Storm Loop"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "no limit",
              "queues grow",
              "latency spikes",
              "client timeout",
              "retry",
              "more load"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Degradation is non-linear. Retries feed the fire — congestion collapse."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Three Tools, Often Confused"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Rate limit: admission control at the edge",
            "Load shedding: admission control under duress",
            "Circuit breaker: guards caller from sick dependency"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Rate vs Quota vs Concurrency"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Control",
            "What it bounds"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Rate",
              "100 rps arrival"
            ],
            [
              "Quota",
              "1M req/month, billing"
            ],
            [
              "Concurrency",
              "20 in-flight at once"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Little's Law Ties Them",
          "items": [
            "L = λ × W",
            "Concurrency follows rate × latency",
            "Capping λ bounds L only if W bounded",
            "Slow endpoint: cap concurrency, cheap one: cap rate"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Where Limiters Live"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Client SDK — politeness, saves the round trip",
            "Gateway/LB — cheapest, guards everything behind",
            "Service middleware — knows the user and the cost",
            "DB / queue — last line of defense"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Real Anchors"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "API",
            "Published limit"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "GitHub",
              "5000 req/hour auth"
            ],
            [
              "Stripe",
              "~100 read req/sec"
            ],
            [
              "AWS API GW",
              "10,000 rps + burst"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Ask This First",
          "text": "All three are token-bucket-shaped. Name the scarce resource — CPU, dollars, a partner's contract, or a human's patience. That answer picks the algorithm."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Anatomy of a Limiter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Every Limiter Is One Function",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "decide(key, cost, now) -> Decision",
            "# Decision = allow|deny + retry_after",
            "d = lim.decide('t42:search', 5, now)",
            "if not d.allow: return 429, d.retry_after"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. The Key Picks Fairness"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Per API key, per user, per IP, per tenant",
            "Composite: tenant:endpoint isolates noisy routes",
            "Coarse key = wide collateral damage"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Key Traps"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Per-IP breaks behind NAT/CGNAT — office = one IP",
            "Behind a CDN, read X-Forwarded-For or get spoofed",
            "Per-user breaks for anonymous traffic",
            "Layer both: loose IP pre-auth, tight user post-auth"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Not All Requests Cost 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Call",
            "Cost"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "health check",
              "1"
            ],
            [
              "REST read",
              "1"
            ],
            [
              "nested GraphQL",
              "50"
            ],
            [
              "LLM completion",
              "4000"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Multi-Tier Limits Stack"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "10 rps AND 1000/hour AND 100k/month",
            "A request must pass every tier",
            "Tightest failing tier supplies Retry-After"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Fail Open or Fail Closed"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Store down",
            "Result"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "fail-open",
              "product lives, no guard"
            ],
            [
              "fail-closed",
              "backend safe, total outage"
            ],
            [
              "reads",
              "pick fail-open"
            ],
            [
              "money movement",
              "pick fail-closed"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. The Response Contract"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "429 Too Many Requests + Retry-After",
            "Retry-After: seconds or an HTTP-date",
            "503 = overload, not per-client policy"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "http",
          "lines": [
            "HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests",
            "RateLimit-Limit: 100",
            "RateLimit-Remaining: 0",
            "RateLimit-Reset: 27   # older: X-RateLimit-*",
            "Retry-After: 27"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Anatomy of a Limiter (cont.)",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Ship It Dark First",
          "text": "Every new limit goes out in shadow/dry-run mode: log what would have been rejected, learn the false-positive rate, then flip to enforcing."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Mechanic",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Chop the clock into fixed intervals (each minute)",
            "counter[key][window_id] += 1 per request",
            "Reject when counter > limit",
            "window_id = floor(now / window_size)",
            "Pure arithmetic — no timers, no sweeper"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Redis Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "local n = redis.call('INCR', k)",
            "if n == 1 then",
            "  redis.call('EXPIRE', k, w)",
            "end  -- one script, or the TTL can be lost",
            "return n <= tonumber(lim)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Memory Cost"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "O(1) per key: one integer + one TTL",
            "The cheapest algorithm that exists"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The Boundary Burst Flaw"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "00:59.9 send 100",
              "window flips",
              "01:00.1 send 100",
              "200 req in 200 ms"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Limit 100/min, client got 2× the intended rate",
            "Worst case is exactly 2× — any window size",
            "Windows are synchronized across all clients",
            "So every boundary is a thundering herd"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Mitigations"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Fix",
            "Trade"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "offset = hash(key) % W",
              "spreads resets"
            ],
            [
              "100/min becomes 10/6s",
              "kills legit bursts"
            ],
            [
              "use as outer tier only",
              "precise inner tier"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Good Fit"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Daily API quotas, monthly billing counters",
            "Cheap outer tier above a precise inner one",
            "Anywhere 2× overshoot is harmless"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Bad Fit"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A capacity-constrained backend that dies at 2×",
            "Anything user-visible near the boundary"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "cont": true
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Fixed Window Counter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "One Line",
          "text": "Fixed window trades 2× accuracy for a single integer. Only buy it where 2× is harmless."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Mechanic",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Store the timestamp of every accepted request",
            "Evict entries older than now − window",
            "Compare the surviving count to the limit",
            "Exact: zero approximation, the reference answer"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Redis: One ZSET Per Key"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "redis.call('ZREMRANGEBYSCORE',k,0,now-w)",
            "local n = redis.call('ZCARD', k)",
            "if n < lim then",
            "  redis.call('ZADD', k, now, id) end",
            "redis.call('EXPIRE', k, w) -- one script"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Unique Member Rule"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Member must be a UUID or now:counter",
            "Same-millisecond entries otherwise collapse",
            "A collapse means one request rode free"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Complexity"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Axis",
            "Cost"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Space",
              "O(N), N = limit"
            ],
            [
              "Insert",
              "O(log N) amortized"
            ],
            [
              "Evict k stale",
              "O(k), paid after idle"
            ],
            [
              "10k/hr × 1M users",
              "10B timestamps"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Memory Attack Surface"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Unauth per-IP log: attacker cycles source IPs",
            "Each new IP forces fresh allocation",
            "Bound key count with an LRU; cap ZSET length"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Two Hygiene Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Do NOT log denied requests",
            "Else a retry loop keeps its own window full",
            "TTL ≥ window so idle users stop leaking",
            "Delete-on-read makes the log self-cleaning"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Fit"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Good: logins, password resets, payments",
            "Good: expensive report generation",
            "Bad: high-QPS public endpoints",
            "There, per-request memory and payload dominate"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Sliding Window Log",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Low Volume, High Value",
          "text": "The log is the only algorithm you can put on the witness stand — pay O(N) only where the decision must be provable."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Trick",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Keep two fixed-window counters: current + previous",
            "Interpolate the overlap, store no timestamps"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "elapsed = now % W",
            "wt = (W - elapsed) / W   # prev weight",
            "est = cur + prev * wt",
            "allow = est < limit"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Worked Example"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Input",
            "Value"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "limit",
              "100 / min"
            ],
            [
              "previous min",
              "80"
            ],
            [
              "current min",
              "30"
            ],
            [
              "elapsed",
              "25%"
            ],
            [
              "estimate",
              "30 + 80×0.75 = 90"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "90 < 100, so allow — 10 remaining."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. The Assumption"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assumes previous traffic was uniformly spread",
            "That assumption is the whole approximation",
            "Error bounded by burstiness inside the prev window",
            "Cloudflare: ~0.003% wrongly allowed/denied at scale"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Cost"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "O(1) memory — two integers per key",
            "Near-log accuracy at fixed-window price",
            "Hence the default for large-scale edge limiting"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Redis Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "-- HASH per key: {cur, prev, wid}",
            "if wid < nowid then",
            "  prev = (nowid-wid==1) and cur or 0",
            "  cur = 0; wid = nowid end  -- roll",
            "cur = cur + 1"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Sliding Window Counter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. What It Buys And Costs"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Smooths the fixed-window boundary burst",
            "No double-dip: the prev window still counts, weighted",
            "No audit trail — cannot name which requests counted",
            "Escalate to the log only for security-critical actions"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Default HTTP Tier",
          "text": "Two integers, four nines of accuracy. This is the limiter you reach for first at the edge."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Model",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Bucket of capacity B refills at r tokens/sec",
            "Each request removes `cost` tokens",
            "tokens < cost → deny, or wait for refill"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Two Independent Knobs"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Setting",
            "Effect"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "r",
              "sustained long-run rate"
            ],
            [
              "B",
              "maximum burst"
            ],
            [
              "B = r",
              "one second of burst"
            ],
            [
              "B = 10r",
              "tolerates a 10s spike"
            ],
            [
              "B = 1",
              "strict rate, no burst"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Lazy Refill"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "d = now - last        # no timer thread",
            "tok = min(B, tok + d * r)",
            "if tok >= cost:",
            "    tok -= cost; last = now; allow()"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Idle Credit"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A quiet client accrues up to B tokens",
            "Then fires B requests instantly — intentional",
            "This is why bursty clients feel fast",
            "O(1) time and memory: one float, one timestamp"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Weighted Cost Is Native"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A 4000-token LLM call simply debits 4000",
            "GraphQL complexity limits are token buckets inside"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Throttle vs Shape"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Flavor",
            "Behavior"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Throttle",
              "deny now, 429 — servers"
            ],
            [
              "Shape",
              "block till tokens accrue — SDKs"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Token Bucket",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Lineage And Misuse"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The standard traffic policer in networking",
            "RFC 2697 srTCM: two rates, committed vs peak",
            "Misuse: B far larger than downstream capacity",
            "Big B? pair it with a concurrency limit"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Default Pick",
          "text": "For API rate limiting where clients are legitimately bursty, token bucket is the best general-purpose choice."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Queue Model",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Arrivals enter a FIFO queue of fixed size",
            "Queue drains at a constant rate r",
            "Full queue → new arrivals dropped",
            "Output rate constant whatever the input shape"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. One Name, Two Readings"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Behaves as"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Queue",
              "shaper, adds latency"
            ],
            [
              "Meter",
              "counter draining at r"
            ],
            [
              "Meter",
              "mirror of token bucket"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. GCRA — The Meter"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "python",
          "lines": [
            "# store one value: TAT",
            "if now >= TAT - burst_tol:",
            "    TAT = max(now, TAT) + 1.0/r",
            "    allow()   # O(1), one timestamp"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "emission_interval = 1/r",
            "No counters, no windows, no sweeps"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Memory And Drops"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Form",
            "Memory"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Queue",
              "O(queue_size)"
            ],
            [
              "Meter",
              "O(1)"
            ],
            [
              "Drop policy",
              "tail / head / priority"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Where It Wins"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Downstream with a hard fixed ceiling",
            "Partner API contractually capped at 50 rps",
            "Legacy system that folds above a set rate",
            "Background jobs and outbound calls, not user reads"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Leaky Bucket & GCRA",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Bufferbloat Trap"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A big queue looks generous, hides huge tail latency",
            "Requests dequeue long after the client gave up",
            "Always pair the queue form with a deadline",
            "Fast-fail anything past its client timeout"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Origin",
          "items": [
            "Standardized in ATM for cell rate conformance",
            "GCRA = Generic Cell Rate Algorithm",
            "Why redis-cell and throttled ship the meter form"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. The Table",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Algo",
            "Mem",
            "Accuracy",
            "Burst"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Fixed win",
              "O(1)",
              "poor, 2×",
              "yes, by accident"
            ],
            [
              "Sliding log",
              "O(N)",
              "exact",
              "no"
            ],
            [
              "Sliding ctr",
              "O(1)",
              "~99.99%",
              "minimal"
            ],
            [
              "Token bkt",
              "O(1)",
              "exact to model",
              "yes, up to B"
            ],
            [
              "Leaky/GCRA",
              "O(1) meter",
              "exact",
              "no, smooth"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Best For"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Algo",
            "Best fit"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Fixed win",
              "cheap coarse quotas"
            ],
            [
              "Sliding log",
              "low-volume security limits"
            ],
            [
              "Sliding ctr",
              "high-QPS edge default"
            ],
            [
              "Token bkt",
              "bursty clients, weighted cost"
            ],
            [
              "Leaky/GCRA",
              "fixed-capacity downstream"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Felt Difference"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Only the leaky queue form adds waiting time",
            "Token bucket feels fastest — bursts sail through",
            "Leaky queue feels slowest but never spikes",
            "Fixed window feels erratic near boundaries",
            "Only the log reconstructs what was counted"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Two-Line Model",
          "text": "Token bucket asks 'do I have credit saved up?' Leaky asks 'is it my turn yet?' Sliding window asks 'how many have I done recently?'"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Layer, Don't Choose"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Edge per IP → sliding counter",
            "Per API key → token bucket",
            "Downstream contract → GCRA / leaky",
            "Monthly quota → fixed window"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Comparison & Going Distributed",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. N Servers Means N × L"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Each node with local limiter L enforces N×L total",
            "The limit silently multiplies by fleet size",
            "Divide by N? breaks on uneven LB and autoscaling",
            "Idle nodes hoard share while others reject traffic"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. One Shared Counter"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Redis or Memcached holds the count",
            "One round trip per decision, every node",
            "Atomicity mandatory — read-then-write over-admits",
            "Lua script or INCR; never GET followed by SET"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "-- one script = one atomic decision",
            "local n = redis.call('INCR', k)",
            "if n==1 then redis.call('PEXPIRE',k,w) end"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Latency Tax",
          "items": [
            "Same-AZ hop: 0.2–2 ms per request",
            "Cross-AZ or cross-region: limiter is the bottleneck"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Redis Is A SPOF",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Fail-closed turns a cache incident into a full outage",
            "Set a tight client timeout — a few milliseconds",
            "Document the fallback before you need it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Sharding & Replication"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Limiter keys are independent, so sharding spreads load",
            "A hot tenant still lands on one shard: pre-filter locally",
            "Async replicas serve stale counts — read the primary",
            "Cluster: one owner per key, no cross-slot Lua"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Cut The Round Trips"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Design",
            "Trade"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Local + global tier",
              "bounded accuracy loss"
            ],
            [
              "Gossip / broadcast",
              "overshoot ≈ sync interval"
            ],
            [
              "Per-region cells",
              "global = sum of regions"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two-tier: node holds its share, syncs consumed counts",
            "Cloudflare and Kong use the gossip style",
            "Cross-region consensus is never worth the latency"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Productionized Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Envoy global rate limit service over gRPC RLS",
            "Istio / Kubernetes wire straight into it",
            "One dedicated ratelimit service in front of Redis"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. One Clock Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "local t = redis.call('TIME')",
            "local now = t[1] + t[2]/1e6",
            "-- node skew corrupts TAT and windows"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Shared State & Backpressure",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "6. Backoff Rules"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A limiter is a contract: server rejects, client backs off",
            "wait = base × 2^attempt, always capped",
            "Full jitter: sleep = random(0, backoff)",
            "Server Retry-After beats the client's own guess"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "7. Budgets & Safety"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Retry budget: cap retries at ~10% of successes",
            "Beats per-request counts when an outage is broad",
            "Idempotency key before retrying any write",
            "Never retry 400 / 401 / 403"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "8. 429 Is Not 503"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Code",
            "Client action"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "429",
              "policy hit — back off, continue"
            ],
            [
              "503",
              "overloaded — back off hard, failover"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "AIMD Client",
          "items": [
            "Additively increase send rate on success",
            "Multiplicatively halve it on 429",
            "Converges on the server's real capacity",
            "A local bucket kills the doomed round trip first"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Key Mistakes",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mistake",
            "Why it bites"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "IP-only keys",
              "NAT + free proxy rotation"
            ],
            [
              "Blind XFF trust",
              "spoofed = no limit"
            ],
            [
              "Unbounded keys",
              "store memory exhausted"
            ],
            [
              "One limit, all routes",
              "export ≠ key lookup"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "2. Counting Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Counting rejects locks a retry loop out forever",
            "Count accepted work, not attempts",
            "GET-then-SET races; a lost EXPIRE leaks keys",
            "No jitter → every client stampedes at reset"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "3. Policy Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Limit set by guess instead of load test + p99",
            "Forgot health checks, webhooks, service-to-service",
            "Throttled monitoring makes an incident invisible",
            "Fail-closed everywhere caps product availability"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "4. Visibility Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No RateLimit-* headers: clients cannot self-pace",
            "They learn the limit only by being rejected",
            "No dry run, no metrics — the alert is a customer"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
